==========================================
SYSADMIN DAY 04 — IPTABLES PART 1
Tables, Chains & Basic Rules
==========================================

--- CHECK INSTALL & CURRENT STATE ---
which iptables
sudo iptables -L -v -n              # list rules, readable (packets/bytes seen, verbose, numeric)
sudo iptables -S                     # list rules as the exact iptables commands that built them
sudo iptables -L INPUT -v -n --line-numbers   # one chain only, with line numbers (needed to delete by number)

--- TABLES (-t) ---
sudo iptables -t filter -L -v -n     # filter = default table, ACCEPT/DROP decisions (3 chains: INPUT/OUTPUT/FORWARD)
sudo iptables -t nat -L -v -n         # nat = address translation rules (Day 05 topic)
sudo iptables -t mangle -L -v -n       # mangle = packet modification (rare, not covered here)

--- THE 3 BUILT-IN CHAINS IN filter ---
INPUT       traffic destined FOR this machine
OUTPUT      traffic originating FROM this machine
FORWARD     traffic passing THROUGH this machine (only relevant if it's routing for others)

--- ADDING RULES ---
sudo iptables -A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT        # -A = append (add to the END of the chain)
sudo iptables -A INPUT -p icmp -j DROP         # -p = match this protocol
sudo iptables -I INPUT 1 -p icmp -j ACCEPT      # -I = insert at a specific position (1 = top)

--- COMMON MATCH OPTIONS ---
-i <iface>        incoming interface (e.g. -i lo, -i eth0)
-o <iface>         outgoing interface
-p <protocol>       tcp / udp / icmp
-s <ip>              source address
-d <ip>               destination address
--dport <port>         destination port (needs -p tcp or -p udp first)
--sport <port>          source port

--- TARGETS (what to DO with a matching packet) ---
ACCEPT     let it through
DROP        silently discard (sender gets no response, just a timeout)
REJECT       discard AND tell the sender (e.g. "destination unreachable") - different from DROP

--- REMOVING RULES ---
sudo iptables -D INPUT <line-number>     # delete ONE rule by its line number (check with --line-numbers first)
sudo iptables -D INPUT -p icmp -j DROP    # delete by matching the exact rule spec instead of a number
sudo iptables -F                           # FLUSH: delete every rule in the current table, back to empty
sudo iptables -F INPUT                      # flush just one chain

--- RESETTING COUNTERS ---
sudo iptables -Z            # zero out packet/byte counters (doesn't touch the rules themselves)

--- WHY ORDER MATTERS ---
iptables checks rules TOP TO BOTTOM and stops at the FIRST match. A DROP rule followed
by an ACCEPT rule for the same traffic never reaches the ACCEPT - the DROP already fired.
-I (insert) puts a rule ABOVE existing ones; -A (append) puts it at the BOTTOM.

